    "jays": {
      "full_name": "Toronto Blue Jays",
      "league": "MLB",
      "record": "33-36",
      "detail": "3rd in AL East",
      "lotl": {
        "label": "What's Going On With the Jays",
        "body": "The Jays keep finding new ways to make the same old point: this is a club that can flash enough life to tease you, then cough up the night anyway. <strong>Wednesday's 7-4 loss to the Phillies</strong> fit the pattern perfectly - Max Scherzer made history by becoming the 11th pitcher in MLB history to reach 3,500 strikeouts, then got tagged for five runs in 3 1/3 innings as the Blue Jays fell to 33-36, third in the AL East and nine games back. That's the hard truth of this first half: a team sitting 8th in the playoff seed line, but with a minus-1.5 points profile, can't afford many nights where the headline is a milestone and the result is still a thud. Scherzer's return from the injured list was supposed to steady the rotation; instead, his 10.23 ERA tells you how much work remains. <strong>Friday night's date with the Yankees in Toronto is the next measuring stick</strong> - and with Shane Bieber due for a rehab outing Thursday, the Jays need help, health, and a cleaner script.",
        "updated": "Updated June 11, 2026"
      },
